LLM inference is the process of using a pre-trained Large Language Model (LLM) to generate text, code, or answers based on a user's input prompt. Unlike training, which builds and updates model weights, inference is a read-only, forward-pass operation.

How LLM Inference Works

The inference process happens in two main stages:
• Tokenization: The input text is broken down into smaller pieces called tokens.
• Prefill Phase: The model processes the entire input prompt in parallel across its transformer layers. This stage is compute-bound and measures Time to First Token (TTFT). It also builds the initial Key-Value (KV) cache.
• Decode Phase: The model generates output tokens sequentially, one at a time, in an autoregressive loop. Each new token depends on the previous ones. This stage is memory-bandwidth bound and measures Tokens Per Output Token (TPOT).

Key Performance Bottlenecks

• Memory Bandwidth: Because decoding happens one token at a time, moving model parameters and KV cache data from GPU memory to the processor limits speed more than raw math capabilities.
• GPU Memory (VRAM): Large models and long user conversations require massive amounts of memory to hold weights and KV cache states.

Common Optimization Techniques

• KV Caching: Stores past attention calculations so the model does not have to recompute earlier tokens.
• Quantization: Reduces the numerical precision of model weights (e.g., from 16-bit to 4-bit or 8-bit) to save memory and increase speed.
• Continuous Batching: Schedules and groups incoming and ongoing requests at the iteration level instead of waiting for entire static batches to finish.
• PagedAttention / Paging: Manages KV cache memory in non-contiguous blocks (similar to operating system virtual memory) to eliminate memory fragmentation.
• Specialized Engines: High-performance serving runtimes like NVIDIA TensorRT-LLM or on-device solutions like Google AI Edge MediaPipe LLM Inference API extract maximum hardware efficiency.
